---
// CAREERS — new page, 2026-09-07. The five open roles were the tail of /about, which is the wrong
// home: somebody looking for work does not read a company page to the bottom to find them, and
// their presence there pushed the actual company story down the page.
//
// Every role keeps its TRUE commitment shape in the meta line — equity, a single engagement, one
// consultation, a research partnership — because this is a pre-revenue, unfunded company and
// presenting a one-off consultation as a salaried post is the one claim on this page a reader
// could act on and be wrong about.
//
// Vocabulary updated in transit: "features" -> "applications", per the product reframe.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PageBanner from '../components/PageBanner.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import FormField from '../components/FormField.astro';

// OPEN POSITIONS. Rewritten 2026-09-09.
//
// WHAT CHANGED AND WHY. The previous version read as a proposal rather than a job page. It opened
// "Five people would change what this company can do", said "what is missing is a second person",
// described a role as "not a hire — the other half of the company", and apologised for the size
// of the list. Three problems with that: it disclosed headcount, which is nobody's business on a
// careers page and reads as instability to a candidate; it inverted the relationship, asking to be
// joined rather than hiring; and it undercut every role by leading with what had not been done yet.
//
// The substance is unchanged — the same research questions, the same regulatory filing experience,
// the same partnership terms. What changed is the frame: each role now states what it owns and
// what it requires, in that order, the way a role at a company anyone wants to join is written.
// No headcount, no counts of what is missing, no apologising for the stage.
const roles = [
	{
		id: 'dataset-partner',
		title: 'Dataset partner',
		meta: 'Research partnership · no fee in either direction',
		flag: 'Priority engagement',
		lede: 'We are looking for an institution holding data it cannot currently vouch for.',
		body: [
			'Two situations qualify: a labelled corpus suspected of being dirty, or a training run that degraded in a way nobody could account for afterwards. Those are the two conversations we most want to have.',
			'We run the diagnostic and the institution receives the report. The data never leaves the customer environment, because the architecture has no outbound route. That is a property of how it is built rather than an undertaking anyone has to take on trust. Findings are published jointly, or not at all, at the institution\'s discretion.',
			'What we require: authority to approve a research engagement, and a corpus with a real obligation attached to it: clinical, financial, or held under a deletion clause.',
		],
	},
	{
		id: 'commercial-lead',
		title: 'Commercial lead',
		meta: 'Equity · full-time',
		flag: null,
		lede: 'Commercial leadership across the regulated markets this sells into.',
		body: [
			'This role belongs to someone who has stood on the buying side of the table in one regulated domain: inside a hospital’s AI group, a bank’s model risk function, a biotech’s data organisation, or coordinating a multi-site research consortium. Knowing how that budget is released, and who has to sign, is the qualification.',
			'The commercial side belongs to this role entirely: first engagements, partnership terms, and the relationships that decide which market opens first.',
			'What we require: a network in one of the five markets that cannot be built from the outside, and enough technical fluency to hold a CTO’s questions without needing to be the architect.',
		],
	},
	{
		id: 'researcher',
		title: 'Research engineer',
		meta: 'Equity or contract · full-time preferred',
		flag: null,
		lede: 'Ownership of the experimental programme that extends the architecture.',
		body: [
			'Two questions sit at the centre of it. Does the per-component health signal lead the global loss curve, and by how much? And does inter-component disagreement separate mislabelled data from data that is merely hard?',
			'The second decides how far the product reaches, which is why it is scheduled before the build rather than after it. The role designs the experiments capable of falsifying the thesis, and co-authors the result either way.',
			'What we require: published experimental work, fluency in PyTorch at the architecture level rather than the training-loop level, and the discipline to run a study that could return a negative.',
		],
	},
	{
		id: 'regulatory-consultant',
		title: 'Regulatory consultant',
		meta: 'Single engagement · months of lead time',
		flag: null,
		lede: 'One judgement is needed: whether the artefact we produce is the artefact a reviewer expects.',
		body: [
			'The work is bounded: read a specification and say whether the record matches what a reviewer would accept, or whether it is something adjacent they would not recognise. Either answer is worth the fee.',
			'It gates the largest application in the product, so it is scheduled early rather than late. A wrong format is cheaper to find now than after a submission is built on it.',
			'What we require: a filed change-control plan for a machine learning system under the FDA’s PCCP framework, the EU AI Act, or an equivalent regime. Filed one, not advised on one.',
		],
	},
	{
		id: 'data-protection-lawyer',
		title: 'Data-protection counsel',
		meta: 'Single consultation · no ongoing commitment',
		flag: null,
		lede: 'One question to settle: what may and may not be certified when data is removed from a trained model.',
		body: [
			'The question is specific. If a bounded structure trained on a named sample set is deleted, and a measured bound on the residual influence of that data can be stated — what may we certify, what may our customer certify onward, and does any of it constitute erasure under Article 17?',
			'A certificate whose wording has not been tested creates liability rather than removing it, which is why this is answered before anything is issued.',
			'What we require: current practice in AI and data rights, and willingness to put a position in writing.',
		],
	},
];
---

<Page title="Careers" description={"Open positions at Oxiedo — commercial leadership, research engineering, and specialist regulatory engagements across five regulated markets."}>
	<PageBanner
		eyebrow="CAREERS"
		title="We are hiring across research, commercial and regulatory."
		trail={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Careers' }]}
	/>

	<section class="section container">
		<div class="car-intro">
			<div>
				<p class="car-lede">
					Oxiedo is building the architecture that lets a regulated institution prove what its
					models did. The roles below are the ones that decide how fast that reaches the market —
					commercial leadership, the experimental programme, and two specialist engagements that
					gate specific applications.
				</p>
				<p class="car-lede">
					Each one states what it owns and what it requires. The commitment shape is on every
					listing, because a research partnership and a full-time equity role are different
					propositions and should not be described as though they are the same.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="car-facts">
				<dl>
					<div><dt>Stage</dt><dd>Early, commercially open, pre-seed</dd></div>
					<div><dt>How we work</dt><dd>Direct. No recruiters, no screening layer, no take-home</dd></div>
					<div><dt>Location</dt><dd>Remote, with the headquarters relocating to San Francisco</dd></div>
					<div><dt>Reply time</dt><dd>A few days, usually less. Every message read by a person</dd></div>
				</dl>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal">
		<ol class="car-roles">
			{roles.map((r, i) => (
				<li class={`car-role${r.flag ? ' car-role--flag' : ''}`} id={r.id}>
					<div class="car-role-head">
						<span class="car-role-n tabular">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
						<div>
							<h2 class="car-role-title">{r.title}</h2>
							<p class="car-role-meta">{r.meta}</p>
							{r.flag && <p class="car-role-flag">{r.flag}</p>}
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="car-role-body">
						<p class="car-role-lede">{r.lede}</p>
						{r.body.map((para) => <p>{para}</p>)}
						<Button variant="secondary" href="#apply" data-role={r.id}>Apply for this</Button>
					</div>
				</li>
			))}
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="band band--dark section reveal">
		<div class="container">
			<Eyebrow>HOW TO APPLY</Eyebrow>
			<h2 class="car-dark-title">No recruiters. No tracking system. No take-home.</h2>
			<div class="car-apply">
				<p>
					Name the engagement and the relevant work behind it. A paragraph is enough. A CV is
					welcome if one is to hand, and not required.
				</p>
				<p>
					If it is not a fit we say so directly rather than going quiet. If it is, the next
					conversation is with the people who built the architecture, with no screening round in
					between.
				</p>
			</div>
			<Button variant="primary" href="#apply">Apply</Button>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal" id="apply">
		<Eyebrow>APPLY</Eyebrow>
		<h2 class="car-apply-title">One form, and it reaches the people who built the architecture.</h2>
		<p class="car-apply-lede">
			No tracking system, no automated screen, no acknowledgement email written by somebody else.
			A poor fit gets a direct answer rather than silence to interpret.
		</p>

		<form class="car-form" id="apply-form" method="POST" action="/contact">
			<FormField
				id="apply-role"
				label="Which one?"
				options={[...roles.map((r) => r.title), 'Something else entirely']}
				required
			/>
			<FormField id="apply-name" label="Name" required />
			<FormField id="apply-email" label="Email" type="email" required />
			<FormField
				id="apply-links"
				label="Anything that shows the work"
				hint="A repository, a paper, a filing, a system that shipped. One good link beats a CV. Optional, and the part we read first."
			/>
			<FormField
				id="apply-why"
				label="Why this, and what is worth owning here?"
				hint="Two honest paragraphs are worth more than a page of qualifications. For a dataset partnership rather than a job, say what the corpus is and what would have to be true to release it."
				multiline
				required
			/>
			<input type="hidden" name="intent" value="apply" />
			<div class="contact-form-honeypot" aria-hidden="true">
				<label for="apply-website">Website</label>
				<input type="text" id="apply-website" name="website" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
			</div>
			<Button variant="primary" type="submit">Send application</Button>
		</form>
	</section>

	<script>
		// A role card's "Apply for this" jumps to the form and preselects the role, so nobody has to
		// remember which of five they were reading. Progressive enhancement: with JS off the links
		// still land on the form, just without the preselection.
		function wireRoleLinks() {
			const select = document.getElementById('apply-role');
			if (!(select instanceof HTMLSelectElement)) return;
			document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('a[data-role]').forEach((a) => {
				if (a.dataset.roleBound) return;
				a.dataset.roleBound = 'true';
				a.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
					e.preventDefault();
					const card = document.getElementById(a.dataset.role ?? '');
					const title = card?.querySelector('.car-role-title')?.textContent?.trim();
					if (title && Array.from(select.options).some((o) => o.value === title)) {
						select.value = title;
					}
					document.getElementById('apply')?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
				});
			});
		}
		wireRoleLinks();
		document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', wireRoleLinks);
	</script>
</Page>
